WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.580
Ras was the standard example of an undruggable target.

2
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.300
It holds its nucleotide with picomolar affinity.

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.540
The cell keeps GTP near the millimolar range.

4
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.860
So a competitor for that site must beat a very tight ligand, present in vast excess.

5
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:28.300
And the known structures of Ras showed no groove deep enough to hold a small molecule.

6
00:00:28.583 --> 00:00:32.743
Kevan Shokat's laboratory went at the mutation itself.

7
00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:36.303
Glycine 12 has no side chain.

8
00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.403
One cancer mutation replaces it with cysteine.

9
00:00:41.163 --> 00:00:46.563
That puts a thiol on the surface of the protein: a reactive sulphur-hydrogen group.

10
00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:53.347
The group held purified K-Ras G12C in its GDP-bound shape.

11
00:00:53.987 --> 00:00:59.967
It passed 480 sulphur-seeking fragments over it, and weighed the protein to see what

12
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:04.167
had attached. Two fragments attached to most of the protein.

13
00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:10.067
Normal K-Ras, which has three cysteines elsewhere, showed no detectable reaction.

14
00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:13.717
The crystal structure showed something new.

15
00:01:14.437 --> 00:01:20.937
The compound lay in a pocket beneath the switch II loop that no earlier Ras structure had

16
00:01:20.937 --> 00:01:27.937
shown. Once bonded, it tilts Ras toward holding GDP and gets in the way of Raf.

17
00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:34.097
The bond lasts for the life of that protein molecule, so the compound never has to

18
00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:41.837
out-compete GTP. In 2019, Canon and colleagues reported sotorasib, a drug for

19
00:01:41.837 --> 00:01:44.557
patients that bonds to the same cysteine.

20
00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:49.497
Keep this rule: bond to a residue only the mutant has.

